WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:08.840
Narendra Modi's recent visit to
Israel, the first ever by an Indian Prime

00:00:08.849 --> 00:00:12.660
Minister, marks India's transformation
from reflexive hostility to a close

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:15.990
partnership with the Jewish state. While
security and trade have driven the

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:18.119
change, there's also another often overlooked

00:00:18.119 --> 00:00:20.880
reason: Within India, the Left lost the
argument.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:24.810
India opposed the 1948 creation of
Israel at the UN, and didn't establish

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:28.650
full diplomatic relations until 1992.
Both international and domestic

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:32.369
considerations shaped India's stance.
Diplomats worried about offending Arab

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:36.059
countries; politicians worried about
offending Muslim voters. Modi's visit

00:00:36.059 --> 00:00:39.510
reflects a deeper shift in public
opinion. Many middle class Indians now

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:43.079
view Israel not as a neo-colonial
oppressor but as Americans do: as a

00:00:43.079 --> 00:00:46.920
plucky country in a tough neighborhood.
Some are also attracted to the ancientness

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:50.910
of Jewish culture which, like
Hinduism, predates Islam and Christianity.

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:54.620
The Israeli Foreign Ministry found India
to be the most pro-Israel of the

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:58.700
countries it surveyed, ahead of even the
US. Close ties with Israel make sense

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:02.580
to middle class Indians. Today the notion
of slowing down or underplaying India-Israel

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:06.780
ties finds few takers outside
India's rapidly dwindling Left.

